{"title":"Career Roadmap — Rehabilitation Engineer","role":"Rehabilitation Engineer","summary":"A Rehabilitation Engineer applies engineering, biomechanics, electronics, software and human-centred design to develop technologies that help people regain or improve mobility, communication, independence and daily functioning. The field includes prosthetics, orthotics, assistive devices, wheelchairs, exoskeletons, rehabilitation robotics, mobility systems, adaptive interfaces and wearable technologies.","facts":{},"about":[{"label":"Direct alignment","text":"Very High — Biomedical Engineering is one of the strongest undergraduate pathways because it combines engineering, physiology, biomechanics, electronics and medical technology."}],"intro":[],"horizons":[],"sections":[{"heading":"Career Horizons","kind":"horizons","blocks":[{"type":"table","header":["Period","Career focus"],"rows":[[{"text":"2026–2030"},{"text":"Biomedical fundamentals, biomechanics, electronics, programming, assistive technology and device design"}],[{"text":"2030–2035"},{"text":"Rehabilitation Engineer → Biomedical/Assistive Technology Engineer → Senior Engineer"}],[{"text":"2035–2040"},{"text":"Rehabilitation Technology Specialist, Clinical Engineering Lead, Rehabilitation R&D Engineer"}],[{"text":"2040–2046"},{"text":"Principal Rehabilitation Engineer, Assistive Technology R&D Lead, Rehabilitation Technology Manager"}]]}]},{"heading":"What to Build Each Year","kind":"build","blocks":[{"type":"table","header":["Year","Core learning","Practical outcome"],"rows":[[{"text":"Year 1"},{"text":"Mathematics, physics, biology, anatomy and engineering fundamentals"},{"text":"Understand human and engineering systems"}],[{"text":"Year 2"},{"text":"Biomechanics, physiology, electronics, programming and sensors"},{"text":"Build basic assistive devices"}],[{"text":"Year 3"},{"text":"Prosthetics, orthotics, rehabilitation robotics, embedded systems"},{"text":"Develop functional rehabilitation prototypes"}],[{"tex